  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/2/22/Tales_to_Astonish_Vol_1_46.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Ant-Man and the Wasp are up above the city, but all is quiet, so they return to the lab.&nbsp; Jan is clearly bored with the inaction, even commenting, \"Peace! Quiet!&nbsp; How <i>ghastly</i>!\"&nbsp; Hank suggests a vacation.&nbsp; Jan suggests Greece, so off they go, arriving two days later.\n\nThey notice almost immediately that most of the local boats are docked -- that none seem to even be out fishing.&nbsp; When they try and charter one to take them out to cruise the islands, the owners refuse, explaining that \"no seaman will take his vessel from the docks\" because of \"the Monster!\"&nbsp; When they ask for details, one of them says \"Ask George in the radio shack!\"&nbsp; And sure enough, they approach a building with a big \"RADIO\" sign outside and a guy inside sitting at a little desk with lots of equipment.&nbsp; When asked about the so-called \"Monster,\" he mentions that he heard the word \"Cyclops\" just before one radio signal went dead.\n\nUltimately they find no one willing to venture out with them, so they'll have to sail out there themselves (George rents out his own boat -- but they'll have to sail it).&nbsp; Jan is excited about the prospect of adventure... and a mystery to solve.&nbsp; They don their costumes as they approach the island, and sure enough, a huge hand reaches down and picks up the entire ship.\n\nWith the Wasp flying under her own power, Ant-Man mounts a winged ant and takes to the air.&nbsp; The pair of them follow the one-eyed \"monster\" to the \"hills,\" where he lowers the boat down to an inland lake.&nbsp; There, the two heroes find other ships, complete with their missing sailors, all being held prisoner.&nbsp; There are also some strange lights nearby, and the Cyclops seems to be making his way toward them, Hank noting that the Cyclops moves awkwardly.\n\nThe heroes follow, and before long, they come across what appears to be the <i>real</i> reason the sailors have been randomly taken.&nbsp; There are <i>spaceships</i> on the island!&nbsp; And the aliens present seem to be testing the captive sailors' brain impulses, but soon, the electronic interference causes an overload to the heroes' cybernetic devices that allow them to communicate with nearby insects.\n\nAnt-Man is able to adjust his helmet and find \"electronic impulses emanating from their brains\" (the aliens), allowing him to understand them.&nbsp; Within moments, Ant-Man learns that the aliens consider Earthmen \"inferior creatures.\"&nbsp; Another even says, \"Earth should be classified as fit to be conquered and colonized.\"&nbsp; The Cyclops turns out to be nothing more than a robot, built by the aliens to frighten ships away, allowing them free rein to investigate the planet.\n\nJan rushes to help the captives while Hank enters the mouth of the Cyclops to look for a way to deactivate it.&nbsp; Hank is able to override the control mechanism and send the Cyclops back toward the aliens, who are taken aback and surmise that \"Earthlings have even <i>stronger</i> mental control than we!\"&nbsp; They've underestimated them and flee to \"escape this accursed planet!\"&nbsp; \"Earth is too strong for us to conquer!\"&nbsp; Hank has the Cyclops return all the stolen boats and then walk into the sea and topple onto the ocean floor to rust.\n\nA few days later, back home, Jan says to Hank, \"My dear professor, do me a favor please!&nbsp; In the future, let us have <i>no more vacations</i>!&nbsp; I just can't stand the excitement!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "A story that drifts from NYC boredom to Greek boredom, with no pulse in sight.\nThe aliens may study humanity, but readers study the clock.",
  "subtitle": "When Even a Cyclops Sees the Trope Coming",
  "review_text": "[ASSET: right | LetsGoToGreece | width=26%] The opening two pages seem to go out of their way to show that <i>nothing</i> is going on in NYC.&nbsp; Jan's attitude, in particular, is puzzling, as she even describes the quiet as \"ghastly\" and suggests that, due to their efforts, they've made crime unpopular.&nbsp; It's <i>so</i> boring that Hank even proposes they go on vacation, and by the third panel of page 4, they arrive in Greece.\n[BR]\n[BR] It's a leisurely opening for a series that normally has to sprint through its page count.&nbsp; The level of inactivity is actually surprising, since even the Fantastic Four manage to pack more into their obligatory \"fluff\" pages at the start of just about every <i>one</i> of their adventures.&nbsp; Here, the lull feels less like pacing and more like stalling.\n\nIt just goes to show how little material there was here -- that despite the fact that this magazine only devotes 13 pages to the cover feature, Stan and his scriptwriter still discovered the need to fill space.&nbsp; If there had been a story with real weight behind it, Stan could have simply opened in Greece and used a single caption to explain the getaway.\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | GeorgeInTheRadioShack | width=19%] I found it interesting that, even while in Greece, Hank and Jan were directed to talk to \"George in the radio shack.\"&nbsp; Sure enough, they find a nearby building clearly labeled with a \"RADIO\" sign outside (convenient of the Greeks to print the word in English) and <i>yes</i>, an actual guy named \"George\" inside it!\n[BR]\n[BR] How many Greeks do <i>you</i> know named \"George,\" as opposed to some name like \"Pantelis\"?&nbsp; It's as if the scriptwriter didn't trust readers to handle a Greek name.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(shakes head)]\n\n[ASSET: right | WingedAnts | width=20%] Incidentally, with a flying partner like the Wasp, it was important to find a way for Ant-Man himself to remain airborne.&nbsp; The opening pages make it look as if he's flying around with her, but we do eventually learn that he was patrolling the city via his catapult.\n[BR]\n[BR] From a narrative perspective, this was becoming a problem, as he would otherwise be forced to return to his lab constantly to re‑launch himself, so this issue goes out of its way on pages 6 and 7 to explain how Hank will now make use of winged ants to soar around.\n\nIt's a practical fix, but also one of the few moments where the series remembers to expand its own toolkit.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nOf course, it turns out that the Cyclops is a robot designed by would-be conquering aliens, who have descended near Greece, learned of its mythology, and created it to frighten off boats so that they could study Earthlings for the purpose of determining their suitability for conquest.\n[ASSET: center | AliensStudyingEarth | width=67%]\n\nNow that their study is complete -- and the captives no longer serve any purpose -- the Cyclops is programmed to destroy all their prisoners, and with only four pages left to go, the battle portion of our program begins, with Ant-Man diving directly down the throat of the huge robot, while outside, the Wasp keeps the aliens busy, calling on a group of nearby wasps to help.\n\n[ASSET: right | WingsDisappear | width=9%] Though the action is pretty run-of-the-mill, we do at least learn that the Wasp's wings and antennae disappear when she returns to her full size.\n[BR]\n[BR] That may have been evident enough to readers by sheer observation, but it is at least a good thing that the narration <i>explicitly</i> points this out.\n\n[ASSET: left | AliensFlee | width=12%] In the end, with the Cyclops mechanism reprogrammed, the aliens run for their lives, fleeing back to their ship to \"escape this accursed planet!\"\n[BR]\n[BR] It's the standard Silver Age formula:&nbsp; threaten Earth, panic, retreat.\n\n[SPAN: display=block; textalign=center; severity=info; fontsize=large; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Horrors!]\n\nOnce again, alien invaders have underestimated humans, and the [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=day is saved!]\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nBy 1963, the alien invasion story trope is already tiresome... [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(and so is this magazine for the most part).]&nbsp; But Stan Lee didn't seem to hesitate for a moment to completely <i>lean into it</i>.\n\nIt's the kind of plot that feels pulled from a stack labeled \"<i>Use When Behind Schedule.</i>\"&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=large; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Sigh!]\n\nThe story was about as dull as the opening two pages, where Hank and Jan reached the same conclusion -- NYC is so boring that they go on vacation to Greece, only to find shacks labeled \"RADIO\" in English, boat owners named \"George,\" and best of all -- aliens about to invade Earth!&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(And let's not forget the fact that the aliens' study of humanity happens to wrap up the moment Hank and Jan arrive -- <i>excellent </i>timing there!)]\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; text=Thank heaven for Ant-Man and the Wasp!]\n\n[ASSET: left | CantStandTheExcitement | width=15%] The ending panels show Jan pleading with Hank to never again have them go on vacations because she \"just can't stand the excitement!\"\n[BR]\n[BR] Far from being clever, it comes across as sappy and threatens to join Don Blake's near wink at the camera over in <i>Journey Into Mystery</i>.\n\nThat being said, the issue wasn't <i>completely</i> bankrupt.&nbsp; We learned two things at the very least:\n[HANG] * Ant-Man now plans to make use of winged ants so that he can soar around like the Wasp\n[HANG] * Wasp only has wings and antennae when she's reduced to insect size\n\nThese are admittedly new world-building pieces of information, buried amidst the tedious, trope-infested plot, but by and large, my reaction to this issue?\n\nI wonder if I'll be able to \"stand the excitement\" of purchasing another issue next month.&nbsp; Somehow, I must summon the courage to plunk down another 12 cents!&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(<i>Courage</i>, Jeff!&nbsp; You can <i>do</i> this!)]&nbsp; Loyalty is a strange thing...\n\n<b>Final thought:</b>\n[BR] It's curious that Hank calls Wasp \"Honey\" right on the cover, and yet there is never a single moment in the story where there is even a <i>hint</i> that the two are more than partners!&nbsp; The cover promises a dynamic that the story never even <i>gestures</i> toward -- marketing doing work the script never bothered to follow up on.\n\nIt's especially interesting because of all the talk by Hank a couple of issues ago, when Jan first debuted, about how he didn't want to get involved with her.&nbsp; In fact, even in this issue, he calls Jan, \"young lady\" at the bottom of page 7.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=large; fonttype=italic; text=Am I missing something here?]",
  "highlight": "Bored heroes, a Cyclops robot, and a Greek guy named George still can't wake up this Ant-Man/Wasp story."
}